The 10 power levels in addition to HIGH allow you to choose the best power level for the food you are cooking. The power levels are listed below, with examples of foods best cooked at each level and the amount of microwave power output you are using at each Level.
<PIC:Manual24_21>
{{image description: Microwave power level chart with columns Power Level, Microwave Output, and Use. It lists 10 High at 100 percent for boiling water and cooking tender foods; 9 at 90 percent; 8 at 80 percent; 7 at 70 percent; 6 at 60 percent; 5 at 50 percent; 4 at 40 percent; 3 at 30 percent; 2 at 20 percent; 1 at 10 percent; and 0 at 0 percent standing time.}}

If the SENSOR function is too long or too short, you can increase or decrease the cook time by 10 seconds after choosing the function. You can only increase or decrease cook time when sensor is displayed.

Example: If you want to add to the Sensor Cook casserole cooking time.<PIC:Manual24_22>
{{image description: Procedure and display table for sensor Cook. Steps show touching Cook, choosing Casserole with number 2, then touching number 9 for More. Displays show SELECT MENU 1 TO 3, CASSEROLE, SENSING, and MORE. A small table lists Cook Time Less 7, Normal 8, More 9.}}

PIZZA lets you reheat one or several slices of pizza without selecting cooking times and power level.

Example: To reheat 2 slices of pizza<PIC:Manual24_24>
{{image description: Procedure and display table for Pizza. Step 1 says touch Pizza pad; display shows PIZZA, then an arrow to SENSING. Notes say four beeps sound and END displays when reheat time is over, and recommended amount is 1-3 slices, about 5 ozs. each.}}

- When the cook time is over, you will hear four beeps and END will display.
- Recommended amounts:<PIC:Manual24_28>
{{image description: Quantity table for Cook categories: Frozen Entree 10-21 ozs., Casserole 1-4 cups, Rice 1/2-2 cups.}}

